WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.070
So it's time to look at how we make our AI agents traverse the world as they perform their behaviors.

2
00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:12.949
Unreal comes with a system that supports land-based AI movement straight out of the box, that being the navigation mesh.

3
00:00:14.189 --> 00:00:19.510
As we mentioned in our gameplay talk, Pawn is the base possessable class that the Unreal Engine offers,

4
00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.510
but it's also not assumed to be humanoid.

5
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:26.839
Whereas character is the first derived version of Pawn that makes an assumption of the vertically aligned

6
00:00:27.219 --> 00:00:32.079
humanoid entity, and it's primarily these entities that make use of the navigation system.

7
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:36.020
The navigation mesh is generated in the editor,

8
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:42.700
and it queries the environment on generation to understand where AI can traverse and which areas are non-navigable.

9
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:48.649
It can be both generated ahead of time, but also change at runtime to support dynamic levels.

10
00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:55.429
Navigation modifier volumes can be used to apply navigation costs or blocks.

11
00:00:56.109 --> 00:01:00.950
These make areas of the navigation mesh cheaper or more expensive to traverse.

12
00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:07.750
Physical objects placed in the world can control whether or not they affect this navigation mesh or not.

13
00:01:09.049 --> 00:01:12.069
Different navigation data can be built for different agents.

14
00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:15.409
Say we have an agent that's particularly big or another one that is particularly small.

15
00:01:15.629 --> 00:01:22.310
Their navigation requirements, of course, will be different from each other, and as such we can build different navigation data to represent their needs.

16
00:01:23.519 --> 00:01:30.719
Navigation links can be formed between navigation mesh edges to allow AI to traverse spaces where navigation meshes may not exist,

17
00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.200
for instance, jumping from one mesh onto another one.

18
00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:41.379
As we mentioned, the navigation mesh is generated within the editor, though it does not come built in with any level.

19
00:01:41.859 --> 00:01:47.640
In order to generate a navigation mesh, we must drop a navigation mesh bounds volume into the level,

20
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:51.409
which can be found in the place actors window under volumes.

21
00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:56.689
We can then resize this volume to cover the area that we wish to have a navigation mesh generated for.

22
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.230
In order to know if your navigation mesh has been generated correctly, we can hit the P key in the editor.

23
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:09.180
If a navigation mesh has been successfully generated for this level, we'll be able to visualize it in the viewport.

24
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:15.819
In the picture to the right, we can see some of the controls that we have available to us on our navigation mesh once it has been generated.

25
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:21.020
For example, we can control the max slope angle that this navigation mesh will support.

26
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:27.500
Another key parameter to control with navigation meshes is whether the runtime generation is static or dynamic.

27
00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:32.310
Static, of course, will be cheaper than its dynamic counterpart,

28
00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:37.430
where it's important to understand what your project needs and that they may vary along the way.

29
00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:42.430
If we've got a completely static scene, then a static generation nav mesh will be perfectly fine.

30
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:48.310
But if your game has got an ever-changing scene, such as trees falling over or objects being built at runtime,

31
00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:50.990
then a dynamic navigation mesh will be required.

32
00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:58.270
Lastly, it's really important to understand that physical objects in the world individually control whether they affect the nav mesh or not.

33
00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:01.500
So ensuring that these are correctly set up is really important.

34
00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.900
In the picture above, we can see a generated navigation mesh.

35
00:03:05.419 --> 00:03:09.620
As well as that, we can also see that the tables and chairs have affected the generation of that mesh,

36
00:03:09.939 --> 00:03:15.530
so that AI can reason that they cannot traverse through the table and chairs, they need to walk around them.

37
00:03:16.129 --> 00:03:21.169
The navigation mesh system in Unreal supports different navigation meshes for different AI agents,

38
00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:29.060
and it's important to understand that the navigation mesh system in Unreal supports different navigation meshes for different AI agents.

39
00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:38.699
As we mentioned earlier, as can be seen in the image on the slide, we can define a humanoid profile that supports AI agents of a certain radius and height.

40
00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.780
We can also determine other aspects of the navigation mesh generation.

41
00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:47.300
This allows us to support different agents with appropriate navigation meshes for them to use.

42
00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:55.020
A larger AI agent may not be able to navigate through narrow spaces in the terrain, so therefore our navigation mesh should not allow them to do so.

43
00:03:57.189 --> 00:04:04.789
As we mentioned a few slides back, we can also make use of navigation link proxies and navigation modifiers to affect how our AI can traverse across the environment.

44
00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:10.879
Navigation link proxies, as the name suggests, allow us to link between gaps in the mesh.

45
00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:15.520
An AI agent can utilize these links to temporarily leave the navigation mesh.

46
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:22.360
In the example above, the AI agent can use a navigation link to jump off the ledge to get to another piece of the mesh.

47
00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:30.310
As well as linking navigation mesh edges together, we can utilize navigation modifiers to affect navigation in several ways.

48
00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.540
With navigation modifiers, we can do some of the following.

49
00:04:34.459 --> 00:04:37.339
We can block navigation, and this is fairly self-explanatory.

50
00:04:37.939 --> 00:04:43.019
This will create a void of navigation in the desired area, meaning that the AI will be forced to move around it.

51
00:04:44.399 --> 00:04:46.800
We can also affect the cost of the navigation.

52
00:04:48.529 --> 00:04:52.730
This essentially means that we can outline areas of our nav mesh that are high or low cost.

53
00:04:53.329 --> 00:05:00.649
A high-cost area will be used as a fallback or last resort, whereas a low-cost area will be used as a preferred or priority path.

54
00:05:02.139 --> 00:05:08.339
As an example, we can see in the image above that we can define an area by the wall that is not a desirable path for the AI agent to choose.

55
00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:13.459
However, if we must, it will traverse through that area to its goal location if no other route can be found.

56
00:05:14.449 --> 00:05:22.129
We can leverage this to create immersive experiences for the players, where the AI cleverly chooses other ways of getting to the player that avoid a choke point in the image.

57
00:05:22.129 --> 00:05:28.459
This can be easily married up with the concept of pathing heuristics in the more classical A-Star implementations of pathfinding.
